            <ArticleTitle>Single-site laparoscopy in gynecology: preliminary study of a series of 50 cases.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>548-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1590/1806-9282.60.06.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0104-42302014000600548</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">to describe the initial experience of a gynecology team, at a tertiary care center, when performing single-port laparoscopic surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">this is a retrospective study reviewing the medical records of 50 patients treated at the outpatient gynecology clinic of our institution between June 2012 and July 2013 who underwent single-port laparoscopic surgery. This study was approved by the institution's Ethics in Research Committee.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">the mean age of patients is 37.8 years, ranging from 18 to 70 years, and the most frequent surgical indications were adnexal mass (72%) and chronic pelvic pain (24%). The mean operative time was 94.4 minutes with a mean hospital stay of 25.8 hours. There were no perioperative complications. We recorded two conversions to laparotomy due to technical difficulties during the procedure. All cases of conversion had pelvic adhesions. All operative complications were successfully treated and none were considered severe.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">this is one of the largest case series in the literature regarding surgical treatment by single-port laparoscopy in gynecology and presents evidence on reduction of surgical morbidity and satisfactory cosmetic results. We conclude that single-port laparoscopy is a viable minimally invasive technique, and that it contributes to the construction of a new scenario in modern gynecological surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
